INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (266,1,'2025-04-29 14:10:54','2025-04-29 14:10:54','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Welcome to WordPress. This is your first post. Edit or delete it, then start writing!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Hello world!','','inherit','closed','closed','','1-revision-v1','','','2025-04-29 14:10:54','2025-04-29 14:10:54','',1,'http://blogproject.local/?p=266',0,'revision','',0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (267,1,'2025-04-29 14:15:55','2025-04-29 14:15:55','<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Strategy Isn’t Complicated — It’s Essential.</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over the years, I’ve seen one thing cripple more businesses than a bad economy, tough competition, or even poor marketing: the lack of a real strategy. Most business owners I meet think they have a strategy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll show me a mission statement.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll rattle off a list of goals.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll tell me about their big vision for growth.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Richard Rumelt nails it when he says: Good strategy is about focus. It’s about diagnosing the real challenge, creating a clear plan to tackle it, and taking decisive action. Bad strategy? It’s a collection of vague hopes and buzzwords that make you feel good but change nothing. And no strategy at all? That’s even worse. It’s chaos. It’s reacting instead of leading. It’s running faster and faster toward nowhere.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this blog, I’m going to break down what a good strategy looks like, why bad strategy is so dangerous (but so common), and why having no strategy is like trying to win a race without a finish line. And more importantly, I’ll show you how you can start building a real, working strategy today—no corporate jargon, no complicated models, just straight-up clarity and action.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Bad strategy burns energy, money, and morale.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">1: What Is Good Strategy? (And Why It Works).</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let’s get one thing straight: good strategy isn’t about sounding clever. It’s not about fancy mission statements, five-year plans, or buzzwords like “synergy” or “innovation leadership.” Good strategy is simple. It’s direct. And most importantly, it’s brutally honest about what’s standing in your way, and how you’re going to break through it. Richard Rumelt defines good strategy as having three essential parts:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Diagnosis of the critical challenge.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Guiding Policy to tackle the challenge.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Coherent Actions that deliver the solution.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let’s break each of those down.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">2: What Bad Strategy Looks Like (And Why It’s So Common).</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Here’s the hard truth most people don’t want to admit:&nbsp;<strong>Bad strategy is everywhere.</strong>&nbsp;It’s in big companies, small businesses, start-ups, and solo operators. And what makes it so dangerous is that bad strategy often&nbsp;<em>looks</em>&nbsp;impressive on the surface. It sounds ambitious. It feels energetic. But when you dig deeper, you find… nothing. No substance. No real plan. Just a wish list.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Richard Rumelt calls this out clearly:&nbsp;<strong>Bad strategy is full of fluff, vague slogans, and magical thinking.&nbsp;</strong>It ignores real problems and replaces action with hopeful declarations. Let’s look at exactly what bad strategy looks like—and why it’s so easy to fall into the trap.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:image {\"id\":73,\"sizeSlug\":\"full\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full\"><img src=\"http://blogproject.local/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/pic-6.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-73\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image --></div>\n<!-- /wp:column -->\n\n<!-- wp:column -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:image {\"id\":74,\"sizeSlug\":\"full\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full\"><img src=\"http://blogproject.local/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/pic-13.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-74\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image --></div>\n<!-- /wp:column --></div>\n<!-- /wp:columns -->','Good Strategy, Bad Strategy, No Strategy: Why Most Businesses Fail Before They Begin','','publish','open','open','','good-strategy-bad-strategy-no-strategy-why-most-businesses-fail-before-they-begin','','','2025-04-29 15:28:36','2025-04-29 15:28:36','',0,'http://blogproject.local/?p=267',0,'post','',0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (268,1,'2025-04-29 14:13:52','2025-04-29 14:13:52','<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Strategy Isn’t Complicated — It’s Essential.</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over the years, I’ve seen one thing cripple more businesses than a bad economy, tough competition, or even poor marketing: the lack of a real strategy. Most business owners I meet think they have a strategy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll show me a mission statement.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll rattle off a list of goals.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll tell me about their big vision for growth.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But here’s the problem: none of those things are strategy. They’re important, sure. They have their place. But real strategy—the kind that actually moves a business forward—is something very different.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Richard Rumelt nails it when he says: Good strategy is about focus. It’s about diagnosing the real challenge, creating a clear plan to tackle it, and taking decisive action. Bad strategy? It’s a collection of vague hopes and buzzwords that make you feel good but change nothing. And no strategy at all? That’s even worse. It’s chaos. It’s reacting instead of leading. It’s running faster and faster toward nowhere.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That’s what I want for you, too. Because if you don’t get the strategy right, nothing else you do will save you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Not your branding.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Not your ads.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Not your grit and hustle.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Without a good strategy, you’re building your business on sand.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this blog, I’m going to break down what a good strategy looks like, why bad strategy is so dangerous (but so common), and why having no strategy is like trying to win a race without a finish line. And more importantly, I’ll show you how you can start building a real, working strategy today—no corporate jargon, no complicated models, just straight-up clarity and action.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->','Good Strategy, Bad Strategy, No Strategy: Why Most Businesses Fail Before They Begin','','inherit','closed','closed','','267-revision-v1','','','2025-04-29 14:13:52','2025-04-29 14:13:52','',267,'http://blogproject.local/?p=268',0,'revision','',0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (269,1,'2025-04-29 14:15:55','2025-04-29 14:15:55','<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Strategy Isn’t Complicated — It’s Essential.</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over the years, I’ve seen one thing cripple more businesses than a bad economy, tough competition, or even poor marketing: the lack of a real strategy. Most business owners I meet think they have a strategy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll show me a mission statement.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll rattle off a list of goals.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll tell me about their big vision for growth.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But here’s the problem: none of those things are strategy. They’re important, sure. They have their place. But real strategy—the kind that actually moves a business forward—is something very different.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Richard Rumelt nails it when he says: Good strategy is about focus. It’s about diagnosing the real challenge, creating a clear plan to tackle it, and taking decisive action. Bad strategy? It’s a collection of vague hopes and buzzwords that make you feel good but change nothing. And no strategy at all? That’s even worse. It’s chaos. It’s reacting instead of leading. It’s running faster and faster toward nowhere.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this blog, I’m going to break down what a good strategy looks like, why bad strategy is so dangerous (but so common), and why having no strategy is like trying to win a race without a finish line. And more importantly, I’ll show you how you can start building a real, working strategy today—no corporate jargon, no complicated models, just straight-up clarity and action.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Bad strategy burns energy, money, and morale.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Good Strategy, Bad Strategy, No Strategy: Why Most Businesses Fail Before They Begin','','inherit','closed','closed','','267-revision-v1','','','2025-04-29 14:15:55','2025-04-29 14:15:55','',267,'http://blogproject.local/?p=269',0,'revision','',0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (271,1,'2025-04-29 14:17:54','2025-04-29 14:17:54','<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Strategy Isn’t Complicated — It’s Essential.</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over the years, I’ve seen one thing cripple more businesses than a bad economy, tough competition, or even poor marketing: the lack of a real strategy. Most business owners I meet think they have a strategy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll show me a mission statement.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll rattle off a list of goals.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>They’ll tell me about their big vision for growth.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But here’s the problem: none of those things are strategy. They’re important, sure. They have their place. But real strategy—the kind that actually moves a business forward—is something very different.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Richard Rumelt nails it when he says: Good strategy is about focus. It’s about diagnosing the real challenge, creating a clear plan to tackle it, and taking decisive action. Bad strategy? It’s a collection of vague hopes and buzzwords that make you feel good but change nothing. And no strategy at all? That’s even worse. It’s chaos. It’s reacting instead of leading. It’s running faster and faster toward nowhere.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this blog, I’m going to break down what a good strategy looks like, why bad strategy is so dangerous (but so common), and why having no strategy is like trying to win a race without a finish line. And more importantly, I’ll show you how you can start building a real, working strategy today—no corporate jargon, no complicated models, just straight-up clarity and action.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Bad strategy burns energy, money, and morale.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">1: What Is Good Strategy? (And Why It Works).</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let’s get one thing straight: good strategy isn’t about sounding clever. It’s not about fancy mission statements, five-year plans, or buzzwords like “synergy” or “innovation leadership.” Good strategy is simple. It’s direct. And most importantly, it’s brutally honest about what’s standing in your way, and how you’re going to break through it. Richard Rumelt defines good strategy as having three essential parts:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Diagnosis of the critical challenge.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Guiding Policy to tackle the challenge.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Coherent Actions that deliver the solution.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let’s break each of those down.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">2: What Bad Strategy Looks Like (And Why It’s So Common).</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Here’s the hard truth most people don’t want to admit:&nbsp;<strong>Bad strategy is everywhere.</strong>&nbsp;It’s in big companies, small businesses, start-ups, and solo operators. And what makes it so dangerous is that bad strategy often&nbsp;<em>looks</em>&nbsp;impressive on the surface. It sounds ambitious. It feels energetic. But when you dig deeper, you find… nothing. No substance. No real plan. Just a wish list.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Richard Rumelt calls this out clearly:&nbsp;<strong>Bad strategy is full of fluff, vague slogans, and magical thinking.&nbsp;</strong>It ignores real problems and replaces action with hopeful declarations. Let’s look at exactly what bad strategy looks like—and why it’s so easy to fall into the trap.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Good Strategy, Bad Strategy, No Strategy: Why Most Businesses Fail Before They Begin','','inherit','closed','closed','','267-revision-v1','','','2025-04-29 14:17:54','2025-04-29 14:17:54','',267,'http://blogproject.local/?p=271',0,'revision','',0);
